{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Who cast the waters of the Sea of Reeds, etc., and HaShem destroyed them to this day</b> — I do not understand the sense of <b>to this day</b>, for all who died in the sea are destroyed with everlasting destruction. And it is possible that it refers back to his horses and his chariot, that Egypt was stripped of horse and chariot for that entire generation, for every horse and chariot and every rider that was in Egypt he brought out with him, and they all perished, and the kingdom was left lowly, without chariot or horse or army or strength. And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 11:4) said that their children never again rose in their stead to be like them, meaning that HaShem destroyed the pursuers in the sea and destroyed their seed in Egypt, and no name or remnant was left to them, for they were more wicked than all of Egypt in pursuing after those redeemed by HaShem. But this is not mentioned in the Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:47:44.732874893Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}